draw the console's own bezel without redrawing it

Upstream's per-platform bezel is a full-frame image with a hole where the
picture goes, drawn over the game pass every frame. A full-frame blit is
345,600 pixels; this device has about a millisecond of headroom at 60 fps, so
every frame is not a price it can pay.

It does not have to. The band the bezel covers is the paper around the
picture, and the paper never changes: only the picture does. So the game pass
is cropped to the picture itself (`Game::crop`, set from `set_game_bezel`) and
the bezel is composited into the surround -- the panel-sized image painted when
the output rect moves and left alone otherwise, where the Advance SP shell
already lives. In play the bezel costs nothing per frame.

The canvas path still draws it per frame, as upstream does: a menu is up, the
canvas covers the panel, and the surround is not in view. Those frames belong
to a menu rather than to a game.

A still (`Draw::Shot`, the frame kept behind a menu) is drawn at the whole
frame, paper and all, so it is not cropped.

`the_picture_fills_the_hole_in_the_art` pins the crop to the art: the hole is
measured off bezel_gb.png and the crop is arithmetic, and nothing lines them
up at run time.

This patch used to carry the other half of the problem too -- growing a
720x480 bezel to a 752x560 panel by extending the plastic at its edges rather
than stretching the art, so the hole stays on the picture. Upstream has since
done that itself, in `backdrop::panel_bezel` (ee19b7a), sized from the panel
and ping-ponging the edge rows outward exactly as this did. That code is gone
from here and what remains is only the part that is about this backend: not
redrawing what has not changed.

Applies to upstream f4b7bc3.

